NNAAMMEE
     aappmm, zzzzzz, ZZZZZZ — Advanced Power Management control program

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aappmm [--AAaabbHHLLllmmPPSSvvZZzz] [--ff _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e]
     zzzzzz [--SSZZzz] [--ff _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e]
     ZZZZZZ [--SSZZzz] [--ff _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     aappmm communicates with the Advanced Power Management daemon, apmd(8), mak‐
     ing requests of it for current power status or to place the system into a
     suspend or stand-by state.  With no flags, aappmm displays the current power
     management state in verbose form.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA      Switch to automatic performance adjustment mode.

     --aa      Display the external charger (AC) status.  0 means disconnected,
             1 means connected, 2 means backup power source, and 255 means un‐
             known.

     --bb      Display the battery status.  0 means high, 1 means low, 2 means
             critical, 3 means charging, 4 means absent, and 255 means un‐
             known.

     --ff _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e
             Set the name of the socket via which to contact apmd(8) to
             _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e.

     --HH      Switch to manual performance adjustment mode, setting _h_w_._s_e_t_p_e_r_f
             to 100.

     --LL      Switch to manual performance adjustment mode, setting _h_w_._s_e_t_p_e_r_f
             to 0.

     --ll      Display the estimated battery lifetime, in percent.

     --mm      Display the estimated battery lifetime, in minutes.  If charging,
             the estimated time to fully charge is displayed instead.

     --PP      Display the performance adjustment mode.  0 means manual mode.  1
             means automatic mode.

     --SS      Put the system into stand-by (light sleep) state.

     --vv      Request more verbose description of the displayed states.

     --ZZ      Put the system into hibernation.  System memory is saved to disk
             (swap space) and the machine is powered down.  For machines sup‐
             porting the acpi(4) style hibernate functionality, on resume a
             full kernel boot will occur, followed by the reading of the saved
             memory image.  The image will then be unpacked and the system re‐
             sumed at the point immediately after the hibernation request.

     --zz      Put the system into suspend (deep sleep) state.

     The zzzzzz and ZZZZZZ commands are shortcuts for suspending and hibernating the
     system, respectively.  With no arguments, they are placed into their re‐
     spective states.  The command line flags serve the same purpose as for
     aappmm.

     These commands do not wait for positive confirmation that the requested
     state has been entered; to do so would mean the command does not return
     until the system resumes from its sleep state.

     Each system provides methods for waking from suspend or hibernate.  For
     those machines supporting acpi(4) style suspend/resume (or hibernate/un‐
     hibernate) semantics, the wakeup devices for each sleep state are printed
     during system boot in dmesg(8).

     The system will attempt to provide as much feedback as is possible on the
     specific hardware being suspended/resumed.  This includes setting system
     LEDs or other indicators to illustrate progress throughout the sus‐
     pend/resume (or hibernate/unhibernate) process.  Such feedback is ma‐
     chine-dependent.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/run/apmdev  The default UNIX-domain socket for communicating with
                      apmd(8).  The --ff flag may be used to specify an alter‐
                      nate socket name.  The protection modes on this socket
                      govern which users may access the APM functions.

